      "confidence": 0.8,
      "curator_note": "Radical-site displacement/cyclization (rd): the terminal chlorine radical cation Cl+. forms a new C-Cl bond to the delta-carbon, displacing an alkyl radical R. and producing a five-membered divalent chloronium ion. Book draws 2 arc(s); moves 2,3 are drawn (the two electrons of the breaking C-R bond: one squiggle-arc into the forming C-Cl bond, one arc onto the departing R.), move 1 is an implicit homolysis hook (the Cl+. odd electron pairing into the new C-Cl bond) required for electron balance (not drawn on the page)."
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence_mechanism_mod",
      "source_id": "mod_rules",
      "target_type": "mechanism",
      "target_id": "IMS4-EQ4.42",
      "support_type": "computationally_supported",
      "locator": {"page": 78, "equation": "4.42", "figure": null, "panel": null, "section": "4.9"},
      "confidence": 0.7,
      "curator_note": "Cross-linked MOD rule IMS_4_42_rd. Its DFS [C]1[C]2[C]3[C]4[C]5[Cl+.]6 >> [C.]1.[C]2[C]3[C]4[C]5[Cl+]6{-}2 cleaves the C1-C2 bond, forms the Cl6-C2 bond (closing a five-membered C2-C3-C4-C5-Cl ring), moves the radical from Cl to C1, and turns Cl+. into a closed-shell divalent Cl+. This matches the encoded step exactly with the generic chain instantiated as 1-chloropentane (R=CH3). Verified: the cleaved C-R bond and the newly formed C-Cl bond are both incident to the same ring carbon; no mismatch."
    },
